---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { appsBySlug } from "../../data/site";

const app = appsBySlug["qwen38-27b-eval"];
const publicProject = "https://scalinghuman.github.io/experiments";
const projectPath = `${publicProject}/apps/qwen38-27b-eval`;
const gamePath = `${projectPath}/mario/index.html`;
const videoPath = `${publicProject}/assets/qwen38-27b-eval/qwen-local-mario-demo.mp4`;
const posterPath = `${publicProject}/assets/qwen38-27b-eval/qwen-local-mario-demo-poster.png`;
const zipPath = `${publicProject}/assets/qwen38-27b-eval/super-mario-web-source.zip`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Build Super Mario Web Edition for $0"
  description="An offline, one-shot local-model experiment: build a complete Super Mario-style browser game for $0 with Qwen3.8 27B in LM Studio."
  path="/apps/qwen38-27b-eval"
>
  <article class="container prose">
    <header class="page-header">
      <div class="page-header__brand">
        {
          app.icon && (
            <img
              class="page-header__icon"
              src={app.icon}
              width="72"
              height="72"
              alt={app.iconAlt || "Qwen3.8 27B evaluation"}
            />
          )
        }
        <div>
          <p class="page-header__kicker">Local AI · Offline · Browser game</p>
          <h1 class="page-header__title">Build Super Mario Web Edition for $0</h1>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="page-header__lead">
        An informal test ride of local-model prowess: one prompt, Wi-Fi off, and
        a playable web platformer four hours later.
      </p>
    </header>

    <p>
      I asked a local{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B">Qwen3.8 27B</a> model in{" "}
      <a href="https://lmstudio.ai/">LM Studio</a> to make suitable assumptions
      and build a Super Mario-style game for a web browser. After the model was
      already on the laptop, the run used no cloud inference, no API credits, and
      no follow-up prompts.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      Create Super Mario game which can be played in a web-browser. Make suitable
      assumptions.
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      This is a practical, single-run experiment—not a benchmark or a broad claim
      about model quality. The result is still notable: a self-contained game with
      no dependencies, external assets, or network requests.
    </p>

    <h2>Watch the local run</h2>
    <p>
      The capture shows the prompt in{" "}
      <a href="https://lmstudio.ai/">LM Studio</a> and the resulting game being
      played in a local browser session.
    </p>
    <video
      class="project-media"
      controls
      playsinline
      preload="metadata"
      poster={posterPath}
    >
      <source src={videoPath} type="video/mp4" />
      Your browser does not support embedded video.
    </video>

    <h2>Play the artifact</h2>
    <p>
      The generated game is published as-is. Use arrow keys or <code>A</code> /
      <code>D</code> to move, <code>Space</code> to jump, <code>Shift</code> to
      run, <code>P</code> to pause, and <code>R</code> to restart.
    </p>
    <iframe
      class="project-game"
      src={gamePath}
      title="Playable Super Mario-style web game built during the Qwen3.8 27B local evaluation"
      allow="autoplay"
    ></iframe>
    <p>
      <a class="btn btn--secondary" href={gamePath}>Open the game in its own tab</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Verified artifact</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>1,592 lines across HTML, CSS, and vanilla JavaScript</li>
      <li>Canvas visuals and synthesized WebAudio; no downloaded art or libraries</li>
      <li>Two levels, power-ups, enemies, shells, pipes, timer, pause, and restart</li>
      <li>59 passing headless tests covering gameplay and render states</li>
      <li>Runs directly from <code>file://</code> without an internet connection</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Download the source</h2>
    <p>
      The generated HTML, CSS, JavaScript, README, and test harness are available
      as a zip of the original evaluation folder.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a class="btn btn--secondary" href={zipPath}>Download super-mario-web-source.zip</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Tools used</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://lmstudio.ai/">LM Studio</a> for local, offline inference
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B">Qwen3.8-27B</a>{" "}
        open-weights model
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>GPU poor, still useful</h2>
    <p>
      Hugging Face's GPU Poor framing fits this run: personal compute is enough
      to ship a complete prototype when the model is small enough to run locally.
    </p>
    <img
      class="project-media"
      src="/assets/apps/huggingface-gpu-poor.png"
      alt="Hugging Face GPU Poor meme used as context for this local evaluation"
    />

    <h2>Why it matters</h2>
    <p>
      Local and edge models can make experimentation cheaper, more private, and
      more resilient when a task fits personal hardware. This small test does not
      replace disciplined engineering, but it shows how a useful prototype can be
      created where the marginal cost is closer to electricity than metered cloud
      inference.
    </p>

    <div class="links-row">
      <a href={projectPath}>GitHub Pages project page</a>
      <a href={`${projectPath}/mario/`}>Browse the playable artifact</a>
      <a href={zipPath}>Download source zip</a>
      <a href="/">Home</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
